# Problem setup:
Let $\alpha \in [0, 1]$ be a real parameter. For complex $n$, define
\begin{equation}
f(n,\alpha)=(1+\alpha)^{n-1}
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{k+1}\binom{2k}{k}\binom{n-1}{2k}
\left(\frac{\alpha}{(1+\alpha)^2}\right)^k.
\end{equation}
where the binomial coefficient is defined by
\begin{equation}
\binom{n}{k}=\frac{n(n-1)\cdots(n-k+1)}{k!},\qquad \binom{n}{0}=1.
\end{equation}

# Main problem:

Evaluate the derivative
\begin{equation}
g(\alpha) = \left. \frac{\partial}{\partial n} f(n, \alpha) \right|_{n = 0}
\end{equation}
for $\alpha \in [0, 1]$.

